# Hostile input corpus

Acceptance criterion AC-04 (and AC-36 from v0.3): no input file makes the Ostrel compiler
panic, crash, hang or exhaust memory. Every file of the corpus is given to both
`ostrel check <file>` and `ostrel run <file>`; each must end within the limit below with
exit code 0 or 1. Exit code 101 (panic), any signal, exit code 2 or a timeout is a failure.

## Limits per milestone

This table is the only place where the limits are written down (A2-20). `run.sh` reads it.
Times are wall clock seconds per case and per command, memory is the virtual memory limit
of the compiler process. `-` means the command is not run in that milestone.

| Milestone | check (s) | run (s) | fmt (s) | memory (MiB) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| v0.1 | 5 | 5 | - | 512 |
| v0.2 | 5 | 5 | - | 512 |
| v0.3 | 1 | 5 | 1 | 512 |

Sources: v0.1 and v0.2 from AC-04 and the `45_hostile.sh` row of MEASUREMENT 5.2 (5 s,
512 MB). v0.3 applies the 1 s per input of AC-36 to `check` and `fmt` and keeps 5 s for
`run` (SPEC 12.4, binding).

### Step limit

Every case runs under `ostrel run` with the default step limit of 100 000 000 steps (G15,
ARCHITECTURE 3.4), because that default is what AC-04 and AC-52 promise. SPEC 12.4: if the
default `StepLimit` cannot be reached within the `run` time limit on the gate machine, that
is a compiler defect reported as BLOCKER to the chief; QA does not loosen the time limit
and does not lower the step limit to hide it. `run.sh` names this in the failure message.

ASSUMPTION: the margin between 100 000 000 steps and 5 s on the 2 core gate machine is not
measured yet, because no VM exists. It is measured with the first VM build (time of
`run_long_loop_by_recursion.ostl` on the gate machine) and written here.

To check the `StepLimit` path independently of machine speed, every case that expects
`runtime error[StepLimit]` runs a second time with a small step limit and must again end
with exit 1 and that error:

Small step limit for `run --max-steps`: 1000000.

### Node limit

The parser has three limits (D43, D54, ARCHITECTURE 3.4); each one exceeded is a
diagnostic and exit code 1, never a crash:

Nesting depth limit: 256.
AST height limit: 2048.
AST node limit per file: 1000000.

The nesting depth counts brackets and blocks. The AST height is the longest path from the
root to a leaf, so a flat chain `1 + 1 + ...` has about one level per operand. The node
limit counts every node the parser creates, not tokens or trivia (`ostrel_syntax::parse::MAX_NODES`,
no CLI flag in v0.1).

The corpus tests these limits with margins, not at the exact boundary (D54; T1 tests the
boundary as a unit test): `chain_below` (2 000 operands, exit 0), `chain_above` (2 100
operands, exit 1), `wide_below` (about 900 000 nodes in many small functions, exit 0) and
`wide_above` (about 1 100 000 nodes, exit 1). Each function of the wide cases returns a
chain of 100 operands, so at least 199 of its about 205 nodes do not depend on how the AST
represents items and statements. The longer chains (`chain_add_100k`, `chain_and_*`,
`chain_or_5m`, `long_line_10mib_add_chain`) are over the height limit and expect exit 1.

## Layout

| Path | Content |
|---|---|
| `cases/` | committed small cases, each at most 64 KiB, plain text |
| `gen/main.rs` | seeded generator, Rust std only, no dependencies |
| `expect.txt` | expected exit codes per case, taken from the spec, never from compiler output |
| `run.sh` | builds the generator, generates the corpus, checks it and runs the compiler |

The large and binary cases (1 MiB random bytes, 10 MiB single lines, nesting depth
100 000, chains of 5 000 000 operators or calls, invalid UTF-8, NUL bytes) are generated into `$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/hostile/corpus/` (default
`~/.cache/ostrel-target`, as in `ci/gate.sh`) and never committed, because the gate rejects
files over 5 MB anywhere in the tree and binary files are hard to review.
The generator is deterministic: the same seed and count give byte identical files, listed
with size and FNV-1a hash in `corpus/MANIFEST`.

## Usage

```
bash tests/hostile/run.sh --self-test                     # corpus checks, no compiler needed
bash tests/hostile/run.sh --ostrel target/release/ostrel  # full run, used by the gate
bash tests/hostile/run.sh --count 5000 --seed 12345       # more seeded cases
bash tests/hostile/run.sh --commands run --ostrel PATH    # only the `run` half
```

`--commands` takes a comma separated list of `check`, `run` and `fmt`. Without it the
runner runs `check` and `run`, plus `fmt` in a milestone with a `fmt` limit; `fmt` is
refused for a milestone without one. `45_hostile.sh` runs the default set,
`56_hostile_run.sh` runs `--commands run`. The `--max-steps` second run of `StepLimit`
cases belongs to `run`. A stderr text in `expect.txt` is checked under `run` for
`runtime error[Kind]` and under both commands for a diagnostic `error[CODE]`.

`--milestone` selects the limits row (default `v0.1`). The self test checks that every
required case exists (the seven named cases of AC-04, the string cases of RED-B F17, the
`run` cases of A2-16), that every name in `expect.txt` exists, that committed cases stay
small, and that two generator runs produce the same manifest. It also tests the runner
itself against `stub_ostrel.sh`, a stand-in that answers every committed case as
`expect.txt` says: each `--commands` set must make exactly its calls, a wrong exit code or a
missing diagnostic code must be reported, and an unknown command must exit 2.

## Coverage

* AC-04 named cases: `empty.ostl`, `invalid_utf8*.ostl`, `nul_*.ostl`, `random_1mib.ostl`,
  `nest_*_100k.ostl`, `long_line_10mib_*.ostl`, `unterminated_*_eof.ostl`.
* Nesting is satisfied by a diagnostic from the parser depth limit (SPEC 5.0), so every
  `nest_*` case expects exit 1. Long flat chains are not nesting (D43); they hit the AST
  height limit and expect exit 1. Height and node limit are tested with margins on both
  sides by `chain_below`, `chain_above`, `wide_below` and `wide_above` (D54, Node limit).
* Bidirectional control characters (D53): each of the nine characters raw in a string and
  in a comment (`bidi_u*`, exit 1), the same characters as `\u{...}` escapes and other
  invisible characters in strings and comments (exit 0), non ASCII identifiers (E0008),
  and raw characters in `style` text (a CSS comment and a declaration value, exit 1).
* SPEC 12.6 (D68): `duplicate_main` (E0201), `lone_close_brace` (E0012) and `indent_jump`
  (E0013) exit 1; CRLF and one BOM at byte 0 are valid, a lone CR and a BOM inside code are
  E0008; an empty file is E0400. Codes are pinned in `expect.txt` once they are registered
  in `tests/errors/README.md`. SYNTAX has no doc comments, so the bidi case for them is
  the plain comment case.
* Chains of REVIEW_RED_A3 #1: operator chain of 5 242 879 `+` in one 10 MiB line
  (`long_line_10mib_add_chain`), `and` and `or` chains of 5 000 000 (`chain_and_5m`,
  `chain_or_5m`) and a postfix call chain `f(1)(1)...` of 5 000 000 calls
  (`chain_call_5m`, exit 1 because an `Int` cannot be called).
* Escapes (SPEC 12.4): every rejected form with its code (E0004, E0005) and the valid
  edge values `\u{0}` and `\u{10FFFF}`.
* Strings (G1, RED-B F17): string literal inside interpolation, `{{`, escaped braces,
  `\u{...}` edge values, unterminated strings and interpolations.
* `ostrel run` (A2-16, G15, D44): unbounded and mutual recursion (`CallDepth`), integer
  overflow on add, subtract, multiply and by recursion (`IntOverflow`), division and
  modulo by zero (`DivisionByZero`), a long loop by recursion (`StepLimit`), text growth
  past the text limit (`TextLimit`), many live texts past the heap limit (`HeapLimit`,
  D44), and many output lines.
* Seeded cases `seed_bytes_*`, `seed_soup_*` (v0.1 token soup) and `seed_mutant_*`
  (mutations of valid programs).

## Adding a case

A crashing input found by the generator or by `tools/fuzz` becomes a committed case in
`cases/` if it is small, or a named case in `gen/main.rs` if it is large or binary. Add a
line to `expect.txt` only when the spec states the expected result, and name the source.
